#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065307} :activity/id #uuid "82288cda-51b8-5251-be5d-9081504538b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050288 :topic/id #uuid "3d4e5778-2967-523f-a9d6-60eceb4cd1b3" :topic/math-academy-id 368} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065380 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the lengths of the sides of a square are growing at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e791c7c33636ef3b2b798fde58825cf4cf1f76e3b6abdd2d92317aa555e1420.png)\n\nIf the square is growing, then its area is also increasing. Our question is, \"*what is the rate at which the area of the square is increasing*?\" In other words, if $A$ is the area of the square at time $t$, then what is\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}?$$\n\nLet $x$ be the length of the side of the square. We know that the relationship between the area $A$ and side length $x$ is given by\n\n$$A = x^2.$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe're told that $x$ grows at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm/s},$ so we have\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = 2.$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2 \\cdot 2x =4x.$$\n\nAnd that's it. We're done!\n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $x$ at that moment into the above.\n\nFor example, to work out the rate of change of area when $t=2$ seconds, we substitute the corresponding value of $x$ into the formula. When $t=2$ we have $x=4$ (from the diagram) and therefore\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4(4) = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "28860e65-09c5-5b95-a728-4cef6731b8b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 478 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ffbfe0ce-ca07-507a-ba31-6478157ea3cc" :step/math-academy-id 886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064016 :question/id #uuid "ec1aaa71-efe4-50fd-8552-bcc98abbd3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-218"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402950}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"} :step/id #uuid "c09954da-b118-515b-b58d-96db87ec4eef" :step/math-academy-id 887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064017 :question/id #uuid "8780fe5c-94c5-5ac6-b6f6-6e86df29b416" :question/math-academy-id "e-219"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373188}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"} :step/id #uuid "9506770d-7ccc-51f6-a401-872306dd8258" :step/math-academy-id 890 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065382 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the rate of change of the diagonal of a square is known, and we're interested in finding the rate of change of some other quantity. For example, can we calculate the rate of change of the area of a square given the rate of change of its diagonal?\n\nBefore we get into that, let's recall a few facts about squares. Consider the square $ABCD$ with sides of length $x$ and diagonals of length $h.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cc3f0cd81cae3bab92ec6c3826f64b7e54e0af61cc32a000569afe5171a27dc.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe also know that the area $A$ of a square is given by $A = x^2.$ So, since $x^2 = \\dfrac{h^2}{2},$ we have\n\n$$A = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.$$\n\nThis formula will prove particularly handy in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae7e4021-bb7a-5e1e-a370-ff99a10aa8a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 481 :tutorial/title "Rates of Change Involving the Diagonals of a Square"} :step/id #uuid "ffbba909-2219-5660-9dd2-f5e847787776" :step/math-academy-id 892 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064018 :question/id #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-220"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398855}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "04a14c93-d5fa-5e41-a14a-97df92f692d0" :step/math-academy-id 893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064019 :question/id #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-221"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048620}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398137}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "df2d4588-710d-52c2-96f4-192142c85391" :step/math-academy-id 895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065311}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064454}]}
